  "teaId": "priscilla-teach",
  "text": "A woman took the most famous preacher in the city aside and corrected his theology.\n\nActs eighteen. A man named Apollos shows up in Ephesus. Born in Alexandria. The text calls him eloquent, and mighty in the scriptures. He teaches diligently the things of the Lord.\n\nHe's genuinely gifted. And then one clause changes everything.\n\n\"Knowing only the baptism of John.\"\n\nHe's brilliant, and he's behind. He's got maybe eighty percent of it, preaching with total confidence.\n\nSo he starts speaking boldly in the synagogue, and Aquila and Priscilla hear him.\n\nAnd here is the move. They do not stand up in the middle of the synagogue and correct him publicly. They do not subtweet him.\n\n\"Whom when Aquila and Priscilla had heard, they took him unto them, and expounded unto him the way of God more perfectly.\"\n\nTook him aside. Filled in what was missing. Protected his ministry while they fixed it.\n\nAnd Apollos goes on to help the believers enormously and convince people publicly.\n\nSmall thing readers have made a lot of. In several places her name is listed first, including this one. Luke never tells us why, so treat the theories as theories.\n\nWhat we can see is that she taught the teacher.",
